{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1480586-need-advice-for-a-entire-session-dedicated-to-chiminage-totem-the-people","id":1480586,"title":"Need advice for a entire session dedicated to chiminage (Totem: The People)","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-05-09T15:24:54-07:00","id":1480586,"title":"Need advice for a entire session dedicated to chiminage (Totem: The People)","content":"Greetings gaian folk, as I said, Im trying to do an entire session focused solely on completing a chiminage. This chiminage is for The people to formally accept to be the totem of the &quot;pack&quot;. <br /> <br /> <b>I will try summarize the background:</b><br /> <br /> -The &quot;pack&quot; are not werewolves, they are humans, a mixed group of kinfolk, kinain and hedge sorcerers.<br /> -They are a good-ish people, with the worst of them being anti-heroes at max. None is really corrupted by the wyrm or anything. <br /> -They become a group because they were tired of being exploited by the more important member of their respective societies (garous, changelings, mages, etc)<br /> -They helped each other and many other marginalized people. Thins like rescuing ex ghouls, helping a kinfolk escape forced marriage, etc<br /> -The people got interested in this unudual group and begun testing them in secret to see if they are worthy<br /> -The most spiritual kinfolk (gnosis 3) of the group recieves dreams and signs from the totem<br /> <br /> <br /> <b>What I want to do:</b><br /> <br /> The people want subjective chiminage. They want the city to wake up and protest, riot against the injustices. The people want broken windows, tear gas and burned cars placed upon an altar of social revolt. It wants the weak and opressed to regain hope and fighting spirit. <br /> I want to make an entire session about the players trying to feed the flames of social insatisfaction until the city explodes in widespread protests/riots. <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>The problem</b><br /> I want to try to make a more unplanned session. I will give them some things they can use, maybe there was an episode of police violence in the city, or the corrupt mayor allowed the water the drinking poisoned, or the governor passed some bullshit abortion laws. Themes they can pursue as they want. But aside from that I will leave them try to achieve the objetive however they want. I will improvise along as they try different things, making up challenges as they go. <br /> <br /> However Im afraid that the players themselves might not respond well to such a open ended quest and just don't do anything. I want a plan B in case that happens. <br /> <br /> What can I do in case the players feel too lost ? How do I guide then, what plan B I can use? <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26021-rucun","name":"Rucun","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"113"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-10T03:55:35-07:00","id":1480652,"title":"","content":"Find &quot;Last Week Tonight&quot; with John Oliver on youtube. Especially for a United States based game there is some social justice to be enraged about in at least every second episode. Create people who suffer from these injustices, then allow the players to be vocal about it. Find gatherings where they can use a megaphone to rally people and find people or places to direct their violence against.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1008-heinrich","name":"heinrich","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1008_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-05-10T09:44:45-07:00","id":1480670,"title":"","content":"In my limited experience as a GM for tabletop games, most of the players that I've had haven't had a great time when I've offered them an open sandbox and expected them to proactively seek out stuff to do; they tend to want some direction. It's a bit of a blind spot for me, because that's what I personally consider ideal as a player. Of course, most of the players I've had have been pretty new to TTRPGs, so that may have been a factor.<br /> <br /> When it comes to &quot;Plan A,&quot; I would try to come up with a wide array of options for the players to get involved with depending on their interests. Environmentalism, criminal justice/police reform, women's rights, LBGTQ rights, wealth inequality, substance abuse, etc. If you make sure to have a potential problem to solve for any given social justice cause, it gives your players more opportunity to find a problem that they want to engage with. <br /> <br /> Of course, looking at the interests of the characters in particular should help you narrow down your options a bit. You should also carefully consider the feelings and opinions of your players as well; in addition to including options that interest them, you should be careful to avoid topics that might trigger arguments among players that may have strong disagreements on the subject, or issues that may strike too close to home for players. Taking the time to figure out your players' boundaries is probably a good idea if you haven't already, in case someone has some trauma tied to a particular issue.<br /> <br /> As for &quot;Plan B,&quot; if the players can't find an issue to engage with on their own, then you can take the lead using an NPC by bringing an issue to them. This can be particularly effective if you use an NPC that one or more of the characters has a connection with, like an Ally or a Contact. The characters may not care about an issue in the abstract, but you can make it personal if someone important to them is on the receiving end of an injustice. If needs be, you can even create tangible consequences to incentivize them to give a damn; for example, they might temporarily lose the use of a Contact if that person needs to focus on finding somewhere else to live after being wrongly evicted from their home.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25886-kharnov","name":"Kharnov","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"683"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}